WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:22.720
NATO, the North Atlantic Treaty Organization,
the most important military alliance in the

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.140
world, has just turned 70.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:32.140
This anniversary comes in the midst of one
of the most significant political and diplomatic

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:37.140
crises it has suffered in its entire history.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:46.850
Today on VISUALPOLITIK, you and I will get
to know NATO a little better.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:55.230
Because... as 2019 marks the anniversary of
NATO, some of the VISUALPOLITIK team traveled

00:00:55.230 --> 00:01:00.729
to Romania to learn more about how this organization
works, to talk to some of the people in charge

00:01:00.729 --> 00:01:02.500
and to witness NATO maneuvers live.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.870
Don’t tell me that isn’t a fitting way
to celebrate its 70th birthday.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:14.299
(We visited the exercise “Vigorous Warrior
2019”.

00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:19.810
It was held in Sibiu, Romania.)

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.720
This video is one of three videos in which
we’ll try to get to know this institution

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.380
a little bit better, we’ll see how it works
today, how it has changed over the years and

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.560
the challenges it faces for the years ahead.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.360
So... without further ado, let's go.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:40.950
On April 4th, 1949, the treaty of the North
Atlantic was signed, which led to NATO, the

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:44.050
most important military alliance of all time.

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:47.550
Throughout these 70 years, the organization
has changed a lot, but most significantly,

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.570
it has grown.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:52.220
You see, it was originally it was an agreement
between 12 countries.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:55.030
Today, it has no less than 29 members.

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:59.990
The United States, Canada, Turkey and 26 other
European states, including several former

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:04.680
members of the Warsaw Pact which was the former
military alliance formed between the Soviet

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.720
Union and the Eastern Bloc countries.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:17.489
Very soon, The Republic of North Macedonia
will become the 30th member of the organization.

00:02:17.489 --> 00:02:22.459
Folks, we’re talking about an alliance that
represents almost one billion people, half

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:27.040
of the world's economic power and most of
the world’s military spending.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.609
An organization with joint command structures
and troops that train together to be able

00:02:32.609 --> 00:02:46.120
to respond to a crisis in a coordinated manner.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.400
This global defence alliance wields an unprecedented
power.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.989
Because, folks, the 29 countries that currently
constitute NATO, have an accumulated annual

00:02:53.989 --> 00:02:58.209
military spending of more than one trillion
US dollars.

00:02:58.209 --> 00:03:04.469
That is, almost 60% of all the military spending
of all the countries in the world.

00:03:04.469 --> 00:03:07.330
But hold on just a moment.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:10.590
Despite having most of the world’s military
spending power, and being bigger and more

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:15.329
powerful than ever before, NATO isn’t exactly
enjoying its golden era..

00:03:15.329 --> 00:03:19.760
This is something we have already talked about
in several videos on VISUALPOLITIK.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.319
Well, in this and two upcoming videos, we’ll
explain what’s happening and how NATO has

00:03:24.319 --> 00:03:27.889
evolved over the years, the threats it faces
and its possible future.

00:03:27.889 --> 00:03:33.580
But let’s start with a bit of history.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:39.010
(THE THREAT OF THE RED ARMY)

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:42.090
The 1940s are about to end.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:46.870
The Second World War ended just four years
prior.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:53.680
The greatest conflict of all time has left
tens of millions of war victims, not only

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.310
direct war deaths but countless additional
lives cut short due to disease and the political

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.640
brutality of totalitarian ideologies.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.909
The end of the Second World War meant the
end of Nazism, but at the same time, it also

00:04:04.909 --> 00:04:10.389
led to the emergence of a new split that would
divide not only Europe, but also the world,

00:04:10.389 --> 00:04:12.540
for decades.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:16.400
Folks, the Cold War had started.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.340
At that time, The European continent was devastated,
it was economically shattered and its armies

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:23.360
were tattered and demobilized

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:30.810
However, there was an important difference
between Eastern and Western Europe.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:34.590
On the one hand, Eastern Europe was under
the Soviet Union’s control, which maintained

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.600
more than five million soldiers in the region
for many years.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.080
On the other hand, the presence of US and
UK troops on the continent shrunk very quickly.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.140
Between 1945 and 1946 the contingents from
these two countries deployed in continental

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:50.530
Europe dropped from 4.5 million soldiers to
less than 900,000.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:54.950
That is to say, by the end of the 40s, the
difference in favor of the Soviet troops was

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.800
of 5 to 1.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.680
That’s a huge difference.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:05.820
And not only that, at that time communism
was an expanding political force... spreading

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.000
to countries all around the globe.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.750
And of course Moscow was very, very certain
that it wasn’t going to loosen its grip

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:13.750
on Europe.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:14.750
Want an example?

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:18.170
Well… check this out.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:28.490
In 1947, the Czech Republic accepted the Marshall
Plan... but was soon after forced to renounce

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.310
due to pressure from Moscow.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:34.620
Only a year later, in 1948, there was a coup
that placed the country firmly under communist

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:35.620
control.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:38.920
In that same year, the Soviet Union carried
out the Berlin Blockade, meaning West Berlin

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:45.780
had to be supplied by a staggering airlift
of 900 daily flights for months on end.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:52.390
Anyway, folks, these were the circumstances
that created real fear in Western Europe that

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:56.760
the Soviet troops would advance to occupy
new countries, such as the Federal Republic

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:59.680
of Germany, or even the entire continent.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.240
It was for precisely this reason that France,
the United Kingdom, Belgium, the Netherlands

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.450
and Luxembourg swiftly agreed on a mutual
defense clause in the Brussels Treaty.

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:11.610
This was also done in 1948.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:15.370
But fearing that this wasn’t enough; these
countries quickly turned to the United States:

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:18.531
the defense of Europe’s free countries had
to be ensured.

00:06:18.531 --> 00:06:25.800
And that’s how, finally, on April 4th, 1949,
12 countries – France, the Netherlands,

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.650
Belgium, Luxembourg, Denmark, the United Kingdom,
Norway, Italy, Iceland, Portugal, Canada and

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:33.550
the United States – signed the Treaty of
Washington or the North Atlantic Treaty that

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.680
gave birth to NATO.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:41.870
NATO’s first Secretary General, Lord Ismay,
summed up the goal of the alliance as “keeping

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:45.250
the Soviet Union out, the Americans in, and
the Germans down”.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:49.770
Kai Weiss, Research and Outreach Officer at
the Austrian Economics Center.)

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:53.491
It was precisely in this founding treaty that
famous Article 5 was established:

00:06:53.491 --> 00:07:00.150
The parties agree that an armed attack against
one or more of them in Europe or North America

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:02.680
shall be considered an attack against them
all.)

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:06.900
In other words, a very clear agreement: all
for one and one for all.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:12.000
"We hope to create a shield against aggression
and the fear of aggression; a bulwark which

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.740
will permit us to get on with the real business
of government and society; the business of

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.740
achieving a fuller and happier life for all
our citizens.”

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:25.660
Harry S. Truman, President of the United States
of America)

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:29.870
But... it didn’t stop there.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:34.740
In 1952, Turkey and Greece joined the alliance
and three years later, in 1955, the German

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:35.870
Federal Republic joined.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:40.430
And it was soon after that the Soviet Union
and the Eastern Bloc countries, except for

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:43.420
Yugoslavia, created their own alliance, the
Warsaw Pact;

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:48.030
An alliance that unified the power of all
of Europe’s socialist countries:

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:55.050
We’re talking about more than six million
soldiers, 65,000 tanks, 15,000 combat aircraft,

00:07:55.050 --> 00:08:00.500
2,000 warships and nuclear missiles distributed
among the member states.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:05.900
Europe was truly divided, two great military
alliances confronted each other across a line

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:07.320
sketched on a map.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.930
So, at this point, you may be wondering.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:11.720
What has NATO really done?

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:14.139
How many missions, how many interventions
have they led?

00:08:14.139 --> 00:08:18.370
How has the alliance changed?

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:24.590
Listen up.

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:30.020
(A DECISIVE YEAR)

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:35.690
After the fall of the Berlin Wall and the
collapse of the Soviet Union, NATO, an organization

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:39.270
created to face the Soviet threat, didn’t
decrease its activity.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:44.430
On the contrary: it slowly began to have a
much more active role, both inside and outside

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:45.430
Europe.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:53.390
Throughout this process, 1999 was a key year.

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:58.450
In that year, three new countries joined the
alliance: Poland, Hungary and the Czech Republic.

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:02.270
Remember that these three countries had once
belonged to the Warsaw Pact and had therefore

00:09:02.270 --> 00:10:09.020
been seen by NATO for decades as enemy countries.

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:12.400
It was, folks, the beginning of a process
that over the years led NATO to include a

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:16.360
large number of former Eastern Bloc countries
that had been under Soviet control:

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:19.839
Latvia, Lithuania, Estonia, Bulgaria, Romania...
even Albania.

00:10:19.839 --> 00:10:24.820
This huge expansion of NATO infuriated the
Russian political elite, but gave the organization

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:29.339
much more strength while also aiding these
newly independent countries to accelerate

00:10:29.339 --> 00:10:34.400
and consolidate their reforms without fear
of reprisals from Russia.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:38.800
But that, folks, isn’t all.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.710
1999 was also the year where NATO carried
out its first major offensive:

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:47.510
It intervened in the former Yugoslavia to
stop the ethnic cleansing that the Serbian

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:52.020
leader, Slobodan Milosevic, was carrying out
against the Kosovo Albanian population.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:56.390
To stop the Serbian government, NATO carried
out heavy attacks against political and military

00:10:56.390 --> 00:11:02.550
targets.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:23.680
Folks, NATO’s spirit had changed, it was
no longer just a defensive organization.

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:33.690
At any given moment, there was even speculation
of a possible terrestrial invasion of Serbia

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:34.690
with NATO troops.

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:35.730
And, you know what?

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:39.690
It is rumored that the then Russian leader,
Boris Yeltsin was so humiliated and enraged

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:44.170
because NATO had dared to include Eastern
European countries in its alliance, that he

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:45.520
threatened a Third World War.

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:49.180
If NATO’s troops tried to occupy Serbia,
Russia would respond.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:54.560
“I have warned NATO, the Americans and the
Germans not to push us into military action,

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:58.690
otherwise there will certainly be a European
war and maybe a world war.”

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:01.590
Boris Yeltsin, President of Russia)

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:08.430
In a way, years later in 2008, Moscow's threats
became a reality.

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:12.770
Georgia’s approach, by Mikhail Sahasvilli,
to the European Union and above all to NATO,

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:15.940
led the Russian government to unleash a war
against this country.

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:19.960
The point, folks, is that in the last few
years we’ve seen NATO take a much more active

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:20.960
role.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:23.850
In addition to their continued expansion and
the intervention in Serbia, NATO has also

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:31.680
intervened in Afghanistan, Iraq and Libya.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.680
And this has led to huge controversy:

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:35.690
The current debate goes something like this.

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:39.920
Should NATO become an institution that acts
where needed to protect its member states,

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:44.880
even if they aren’t under direct attack?

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:48.940
Should it step in if human rights are being
seriously violated anywhere in the world?

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:53.440
Or should it be limited to being exclusively
a defense alliance, that is, limit itself

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:59.399
to act only if a member country is militarily
attacked?

00:12:59.399 --> 00:13:03.720
In short, does NATO make sense today as it
was originally intended?

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:12.430
And, be careful, because the implications
of this debate are huge.

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:16.330
After all, we’re talking about the future
of the world and the role of the most important

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:19.830
military alliance of all time.

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:25.020
We’re talking about a debate that is at
the forefront and that in recent times has

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:29.190
had the President of the United States, Donald
Trump, as an undisputed protagonist.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:45.920
He claims that NATO no longer works, that
it doesn’t make sense, that it should become

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:48.480
obsolete.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:54.550
And this gives rise to another question.

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:58.250
So far NATO has always worked under the leadership
of the United States, but...

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:02.779
Could it still work and have a future if the
United States stepped down from its leadership

00:14:02.779 --> 00:14:03.779
position?

00:14:03.779 --> 00:14:07.459
Even if the US remained a member, could NATO
be compatible with a new European military

00:14:07.459 --> 00:14:10.410
leadership structure?

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:14.330
This is a topic that we’ve already talked
about here on VISUALPOLITIK on several occasions,

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:19.040
but folks, we’ll take a more detailed look
in a future video.

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:28.529
For now, we can say that despite their problems
and mistakes, NATO has been a huge success.

00:14:28.529 --> 00:14:33.500
I mean, Europe today enjoys an environment
of peace, safety and cooperation between countries.

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:35.940
Come on, it’s like a gang of good friends.

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:38.360
And don’t think that this was always the
case.

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:40.760
Nothing could be further from the truth.

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:44.061
For centuries, conflicts and wars between
European nations were routine events, and

00:14:44.061 --> 00:14:47.880
even after the Second World War, the tension
between the West and the East only flourished.

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:52.080
In many respects NATO fulfilled its purpose
perfectly, even though its member countries

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.920
haven’t always gotten along and have had
their good and bad times, like when France

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:03.050
withdrew from the joint military structure
– not the alliance – in 1966.

00:15:03.050 --> 00:15:06.330
Anyway folks, that’s the story of how NATO
was born, grew and changed.

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:10.990
In a future video we’ll examine what the
alliance means today and what threats it faces.

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:14.970
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
